<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hromkla</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Rumkale</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hromkla, Turkish Rumkale, is a fortress on a crag above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>. From 1149 it was the seat of the Catholicos of the Armenian Church and a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript-illumination">manuscript illumination</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toros-roslin">Toros Roslin</a> worked. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> captured it in 1292, and the catholicosate moved to Sis.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hromkla.jpg" alt="Hromkla" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carole Raddato from Frankfurt, Germany · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Euphrates_River_in_Turkey,_Rumkale_(52031477798).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
